# Parse error: syntax error, unexpected '[' with php 5.3

You are encountering a classic version mismatch issue that often trips up developers moving code from a local environment like XAMPP to a production server. The error message `Parse error: syntax error, unexpected '['` points directly to the line `$item = $xml->xpath($path)[0];`. While this looks like simple array access, it relies on a specific language feature that was not available in PHP 5.3.

The culprit is the square bracket syntax used for accessing properties of an object returned by `DOMXPath::query()`. In your code snippet, `$xml->xpath($path)` returns a `DOMNodeList` object. Prior to PHP 5.4, this class did not support array-style access. You cannot simply write `[0]` to get the first node; you must use the `current()` method or the `item()` method instead.

Here is how that specific line should be written for PHP 5.3:

```php
$path = '//item[@id="'.$id.'"]';
if ($xml->xpath($path)) {
    $item = $xml->xpath($path)->current(); // or ->item(0)
} else {
    die('<p class="error">Script Error: Code 101 - Please contact administrator</p>');
}
```

The reason your XAMPP installation works is almost certainly because it runs an older version of PHP (likely 5.2.x or earlier) where this behavior was consistent, or conversely, if your local setup is actually running a newer version like 5.4+ while the server remains stuck on 5.3. The discrepancy highlights why relying solely on local testing for feature availability can be misleading. Server environments often run strict versions of PHP to ensure compatibility and security, whereas local stacks like XAMPP are frequently updated or customized without checking version constraints.

To confirm the exact version causing the issue, you can add a quick check at the very top of your script using `phpversion()`. If this returns `5.3.*`, the square bracket syntax is invalid. The fix is straightforward: replace the bracket notation with the `current()` method on the resulting `DOMNodeList` object.

It is worth noting that `DOMNodeList` remains iterable in PHP 5.3, so looping through results is also a viable pattern if you need to process multiple items. However, for single-item retrieval, switching from `$list[0]` to `$list->current()` is the only compatible approach for that specific version.

When migrating codebases, it is prudent to check the server's `php.ini` file or the output of `php -v` to ensure you are not inadvertently using 5.4+ features on a 5.3 runtime. Many modern frameworks and libraries assume a minimum PHP version; if your local environment is newer than your production server, code that works locally may fail with parse errors upon deployment.

If you are building a custom application or maintaining legacy systems on older stacks, understanding these historical language quirks is essential. While upgrading to PHP 5.4+ is the most sustainable long-term solutionâsince the bracket syntax has been supported since thenâimmediate compatibility fixes like using `current()` allow you to maintain functionality without an immediate upgrade path. For more modern workflows involving XML parsing and DOM manipulation, consider exploring how Laravel handles these structures if your project eventually transitions to a framework-based architecture.
